\nAnswer: Play W1", "output": "Situation: I am West Wind seat with North Wind as prevailing wind. My hand has 14 tiles, including four potential pairs (W2, B3, B9, F3) and six single tiles (W1, W6, B8, F4, J2, J3), with no exposed melds. The previous player has formed a Character Chow (W6, W7, W8) via Chi W7, and the across player discarded W8, indicating active Character suit play. Unshown tiles show good availability for most tiles, but W6, W7, and W8 have reduced counts (3 each) due to the exposed meld and discards. With four pairs already, Seven Pairs (24 points) is a highly feasible and valuable target, as other high-scoring patterns (e.g., Full Flush, All Honors) are unattainable due to mixed suits and honors.\n\nEvaluation: Discarding a single tile (W1, W6, B8, F4, J2, or J3) preserves the four pairs, crucial for Seven Pairs. Discarding from a pair (e.g., F3, B3, B9, W2) reduces pair count and harms Seven Pairs potential. Among singles:  \n- W1 (terminal) has only one chow possibility (1-2-3), making it safest to discard with low risk of completing opponents' hands.  \n- W6 and B8 (simples) have multiple chow possibilities (e.g., W6 in 4-5-6/5-6-7/6-7-8), increasing discard risk.  \n- Honors (F4, J2, J3) are high-risk discards as they can directly complete pungs for others (e.g., F4 for prevailing wind pung).  \nW1 is least useful in my hand\u2014it cannot form a chow without W3 (unavailable) and is a terminal with limited scoring utility\u2014while discarding it avoids accidental chow formations that could derail Seven Pairs.\n\nReason: To optimize for Seven Pairs, I must maintain all existing pairs and minimize meld formation. Discarding W1 keeps the four pairs intact, eliminates a terminal with poor flexibility, and poses the lowest safety risk. It also aligns with early-game strategy where terminals are commonly discarded, reducing the chance of enabling opponents' wins. Other singles like honors or simples carry higher risks or less hand-cohesion benefit.\n\nAnswer: Play W1"}
